---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Blk from "../components/Blk.astro";
import Note from "../components/Note.astro";
import Scroll from "../components/Scroll.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import d from "../data/searched.json";
import cov from "../data/coverage.json";

const by = (o) => d.rows.filter((r) => r.outcome === o);
const yields = by("yield"), nulls = by("null"), blocked = by("blocked");
const CHIP = { yield: ["doc", "found something"], null: ["lore", "found nothing"], blocked: ["sup", "could not be reached"] };
---
<Base
  title="What Has Been Read"
  eyebrow={`${d.rows.length} sources examined · ${yields.length} yields, ${nulls.length} nulls, ${blocked.length} blocked`}
  dek="Everything this archive has opened, whether or not it gave anything up — because a search that returns nothing is a result, and the next person should not have to run it again.">

  <section class="blk">
    <p class="lede">{d.note}</p>
    <Note>
      <strong>A null result is not a failure, and it is not the same as a gap.</strong> There are
      three outcomes on this page and they mean different things.
      <ul style="margin:.7rem 0 0;padding-left:1.1rem">
        <li style="margin-bottom:.4rem"><span class="chip doc">found something</span> the source was
        read and it gave up a fact this archive now publishes.</li>
        <li style="margin-bottom:.4rem"><span class="chip lore">found nothing</span> the source was
        read properly and the thing is not in it. <strong>This is knowledge.</strong> Four of the
        nulls below are load-bearing: they are why the Hornby claim is called <em>unevidenced</em>
        rather than disputed.</li>
        <li><span class="chip sup">could not be reached</span> the source exists and this archive
        could not get at it — a bot check, a migration, a login. <strong>Not a null result</strong>,
        and it must never be reported as one. <a href={u("/errands/")}>Several are jobs for a
        person →</a></li>
      </ul>
    </Note>
  </section>

  {[["Sources that gave something up", yields], ["Sources read and empty", nulls],
    ["Sources this archive could not reach", blocked]].map(([h, rows]) => (
    <section class="blk">
      <h2>{h}</h2>
      <p class="small">{rows.length} {rows.length === 1 ? "source" : "sources"}</p>
      <div style="margin-top:1.2rem;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:1.2rem">
        {rows.map((r) => (
          <div style="border-left:2px solid var(--rule-strong);padding:2px 0 2px 16px">
            <div><strong>{r.src}</strong>
              <span class={`chip ${CHIP[r.outcome][0]}`} style="margin-left:.5rem">{CHIP[r.outcome][1]}</span>
              <span class="small" style="margin-left:.5rem;color:var(--ink-3)">{r.when}</span></div>
            <p class="small" style="margin-top:.35rem;color:var(--ink-2)"><em>Searched:</em> {r.what}</p>
            <p class="small" style="margin-top:.35rem">{r.got}
              {r.href && <> <a href={u(r.href)}>→</a></>}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>
  ))}

  <Blk label="Checked before searching, every time" title="What each database actually holds">
    <p class="dek">{cov.note}</p>

    <Scroll>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Database</th><th>Parish or series</th><th>Holds</th><th class="m">Span</th><th>Verdict</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {cov.rows.map((r) => (
            <tr>
              <td class="small">{r.db}</td>
              <td><strong>{r.where}</strong><div class="small">{r.note} <a href={u(r.href)}>→</a></div></td>
              <td class="small">{r.holds}</td>
              <td class="m">{r.span}</td>
              <td>{r.verdict === "good" ? <span class="chip doc">covers it</span>
                 : r.verdict === "partial" ? <span class="chip inf">partly</span>
                 : r.verdict === "absent" ? <span class="chip sup">holds nothing</span>
                 : <span class="chip lore">cannot answer</span>}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </Scroll>

    <Note>
      <strong>Three of this archive's oldest dead ends turn out to be dead ends in a database.</strong>
      County Limerick, South Leith and St Quivox have each been called an open question for months.
      None of them has been searched and found empty — <strong>none of them has any data to
      search.</strong> That is a completely different kind of not-knowing, and it points at a
      different fix: an archive, not another query. <a href={u("/errands/")}>The queue →</a>
    </Note>
  </Blk>

  <Blk label="Method, for whoever does this next" title="What these two sites do that will waste your afternoon">
    <ul class="dek" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>FreeREG caps results at 500</strong> and says so only in
      the body text. A county-wide surname search will silently hit it. Narrow by parish using the
      place selector, which only populates after the county is chosen and a moment has passed.</li>
      <li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>FreeREG allows at most three counties</strong> per
      search, and rejects a fourth with a validation error rather than ignoring it.</li>
      <li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>Phonetic matching is a trap on this surname.</strong>
      A soundex search for <em>Saniger</em> on FreeBMD returns <strong>14,300</strong> matches,
      because it sweeps in every SINGER and SANGER in England. It is the right setting for Berkeley
      and the wrong one for Bristol.</li>
      <li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>FreeBMD returns its results in a JavaScript array</strong>,
      not an HTML table — <span class="mono">searchData = new Array(...)</span>, with a
      <span class="mono">" ;0;quarter;year"</span> line before each block. Parsing the page's tables
      finds nothing and looks exactly like a null result.</li>
      <li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>FreeREG's own coverage tool and its search disagree.</strong>
      The contents page answers “Non existent County has been selected” for Ayrshire; the search
      returns 128 Murdochs. Trust the search.</li>
      <li><strong>Control-test before believing a zero.</strong> Every null on this page was checked
      by running a query through the same harness that had to return something — 21,248 Wakefield
      marriages, 202 Ayrshire Smiths — so that an empty result means an empty record and not a broken
      form.</li>
    </ul>
  </Blk>

  <section class="blk" style="border-bottom:0">
    <h2>Why this page exists</h2>
    <p class="dek">Because the alternative wastes somebody's afternoon. An archive that publishes
    only its successes is telling you half the truth, and the half it leaves out is the half that
    would stop the next person repeating three days of work.</p>
    <p class="dek" style="margin-top:.9rem">If you have read a source that is not here, or read one
    that is here and got a different answer, that is the most useful thing you can send.
    <a href={u("/changes/")}>What changed →</a> · <a href={u("/errands/")}>Errands →</a> ·
    <a href={u("/open-questions/")}>What is still open →</a></p>
  </section>
</Base>
